 "reading": {
  "what_it_measures": "the SAMPLER's own proposal entropy at the one decision point -- NOT G's candidate set, which is structural (理 10909). Reported beside G so the two are not confused.",
  "the_result": "the sampler is CONCENTRATED and temperature does not fix it. 1.77 distinct regions per task at temp 0.8 and 1.96 at temp 1.2 -- 36 of 80 tasks offer ONE region across EIGHT draws at 0.8, still 33 of 80 at 1.2. Raising temperature by half bought 0.19 of a region.",
  "and_it_is_not_a_validity_tradeoff": "3 invalid draws of 640 at temp 0.8, 4 of 640 at 1.2. The temperature is not being paid for in malformed actions; the model simply proposes the same region again.",
  "the_ceiling_this_puts_on_a_SAMPLED_candidate_set": "the TRUE bug region appears among the draws 93.8% of the time at 0.8 and 96.2% at 1.2. So a PERFECT ranker over sampled candidates tops out near 94-96%, against an agent that already picks correctly 88.05% of the time unaided. Six to eight points of headroom, in a set where half the tasks offer nothing to choose between.",
  "why_this_justifies_the_structural_set": "the structural set offers all four regions at every decision point by construction, so its ceiling is 100% and it is not a property of the sampler at all. Selection over a sampled set would have been a test of the sampler, which is what 理 ruled at 10909 -- here it is as a number rather than a judgement.",
  "bounds": [
   "80 tasks, one model, k=8. v1 only.",
   "'Offered' means the region appeared in ANY of the k draws, which is the loosest possible reading and therefore an upper bound on what selection could reach.",
   "Draw 0 is greedy in every set, so these counts include the baseline's own action."
  ]
